#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 900.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097430} :activity/id #uuid "a699a755-f56a-5a6a-8493-60b83f42eaa5" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048842 :topic/id #uuid "c54eaff0-4e6f-57d2-aa08-401a88379d23" :topic/math-academy-id 1110} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097430 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097820 :tutorial/content "We know how to calculate the derivative of the product of two functions, but what if we have a quotient?\n\nFor that, we can use the **quotient rule**. The quotient rule is a formula to calculate the derivative of the ratio of two functions like \n\n$$\nf(x) = \\dfrac {u(x)}{v(x)}.\n$$\n\nThe quotient rule for differentiation states that the derivative of the function above is given by\n\n$$\nf'(x) = \\dfrac {u'(x)\\cdot v(x) - u(x) \\cdot v'(x)} {[v(x)]^2}.\n$$\n\nFor example, suppose that we have the function $f(x)$ given by\n\n$$\nf(x)= \\dfrac{x}{x^2+1},\n$$\n\nand we want to find $f'(x).$ \n\nHere $u(x)=x$ and $v(x)=x^2+1.$  Their derivatives are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}^{'}(x) & =(x)^{'}=1, \\\\ {v}^{'}(x) & =({x}^{2}+1)^{'}=2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we plug them in the formula, we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{'}(x) & =\\frac{{u}^{'}(x)\\cdot v(x)-u(x)\\cdot {v}^{'}(x)}{[v(x)]^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1\\cdot ({x}^{2}+1)-x\\cdot 2x}{({x}^{2}+1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2}+1-2{x}^{2}}{({x}^{2}+1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1-{x}^{2}}{({x}^{2}+1)^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "dd2362a7-73d2-577f-aa31-2150f8f6b63f" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2624 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "ed5427f5-4392-5166-a825-dd9dcb07cc3d" :step/math-academy-id 12332 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097431}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097431 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096620 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096420 :question/id #uuid "932c9b44-c040-5ae1-84f7-277767c255d4" :question/math-academy-id "e-3637"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fc978820-517c-5885-b31b-ab6f9650d57a" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048932}] :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492115} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097024} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097025} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233084} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233085} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233906} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235353} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235369} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314921} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314923} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314924} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314926}] :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Quotient of Two Polynomials"} :step/id #uuid "6859d258-9c69-5bab-88e5-4b946166e00d" :step/math-academy-id 12333 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097432}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097432 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096621 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096421 :question/id #uuid "f804809a-22bf-5ab3-9775-3591d4581797" :question/math-academy-id "e-3638"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3c547746-49c8-5a4a-85fe-c6b75493a354" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048749} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050420} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050690}] :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186488448} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097141} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097246} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233908} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234507} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235433} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235481} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290512} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314927} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314928} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314929} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329841} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329849} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329887} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329888} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186488408} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186488413} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186488417} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186488419} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186488420} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186488433} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186514422}] :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Quotient Containing Other Functions"} :step/id #uuid "f384d7ca-2c43-5aae-b786-23e8a903bb4f" :step/math-academy-id 12334 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097433}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097433 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097821 :tutorial/content "Using Leibniz notation, the quotient rule is expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}\\left (\\dfrac u v\\right) = \\dfrac {v \\dfrac {\\textrm{d}u} {\\textrm{d}x}\n- u \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}v}{\\textrm{d}x}}{v^2}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "4ed29747-9007-50b8-9347-89e46eabc182" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2625 :tutorial/title "Leibniz Notation"} :step/id #uuid "185d55a4-49d5-53d5-95b3-8eb93729ad7f" :step/math-academy-id 12335 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097434}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097434 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096622 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096422 :question/id #uuid "53d435ea-a20f-5216-8020-f36cdf8b7c36" :question/math-academy-id "e-3639"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "af269509-92c0-5a82-b828-e8626f8e8eda" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097023} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097402} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233909} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233910} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253590} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314931} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314932} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314933} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314934} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314935} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329848}] :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Quotient Using Leibniz Notation"} :step/id #uuid "f7097f26-3f43-5e1d-8496-783599f1b7ac" :step/math-academy-id 12336 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097435}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097435 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097822 :tutorial/content "We can now prove that $(\\tan x)' = \\sec^2{x}$ using the quotient rule. Here's how! \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{tan}x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}x}{\\operatorname{cos}x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\operatorname{sin}x)^{'}\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}x)-(\\operatorname{sin}x)\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}x)^{'}}{{cos}^{2}x} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\operatorname{cos}x)\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}x)-(\\operatorname{sin}x)\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}x)}{{cos}^{2}x} \\\\  & =\\frac{{cos}^{2}x+{sin}^{2}x}{{cos}^{2}x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{cos}^{2}x} \\\\  & ={sec}^{2}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "584707ea-2c96-5389-926d-b9ac87adc17c" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2626 :tutorial/title "Justifying the Formula for the Derivative of Tangent"} :step/id #uuid "c001eb53-21b0-5423-a50f-1e506d151f8c" :step/math-academy-id 12337}] :activity/title "The Quotient Rule for Differentiation" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186096890}]}
